.TH CLOG_FATAL 3 "2026-07-10" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
clog_fatal \- emit a log line at FATAL level with a backtrace, then terminate the process
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/clogger.h>

clog_fatal(clog lg, const char *fmt, ...);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Writes a structured log line at CLOG_FATAL severity, appends a full
stack backtrace, and then stops the calling process with
exit(EXIT_FAILURE).  The macro never returns to the caller.

exit() runs the exit drain of the library (see clog_create_from_fd_mp(3)),
which delivers what every other async logger still holds and lets every
gzip compression that a rotation of any logger in the process has queued
finish, including one that the fatal record itself can trigger, so no
rotated file is left as a half-written \&.gz file beside its source.
One budget of 5 seconds bounds that drain, and the drain takes the lock
of a logger only when that logger has a queue or a compression to drain.
A thread of another logger that blocks for ever (inside a write to a
pipe that nobody reads, for example) therefore never keeps the process
from stopping.

The same budget, counted from the start of the first fatal call of the
process, bounds the fatal record itself.  On an async logger the drain
of its own queue and batch ends by then, and so does every write of the
record to an output that is not a regular file (a pipe, a FIFO, a
socket, a terminal).  If such an output takes nothing more in that time,
for example a full pipe whose reader stopped reading, the record is
lost: one line on stderr says so, unless stderr is that same output, and
the process stops anyway.  A write to a regular file waits on the disk
and not on a reader, and it has no bound.  The exit drain that follows
shares the deadline, so one budget bounds the whole stop.  While a fatal
call waits for the logger, the writer thread of an async logger takes
on no new work, and that wait may run up to 100 milliseconds past the
budget so that a write in progress on the logger can end and hand the
logger over.

clog_fatal is the one clog_<level> macro that goes past the min_level
filter of the logger.  The log line and the backtrace are always
written, whatever the minimum level of the logger is (CLOG_OFF
included), so nothing can silently hide the reason for the stop.

The log line and the backtrace are written inside the shared write
lock, so they always appear together in the output, even when other
threads log at the same time.

In CLOG_FMT_JSON mode the backtrace goes into the JSON object as an
inline "bt" array, as it does for clog_error and clog_alert.  In logfmt
mode it follows the main record as continuation lines indented by a
tab.  In CLOG_FMT_SYSLOG mode each frame is a separate syslog message
that carries the same PRI, TIMESTAMP and MSGID as the main record.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
lg     Logger handle (clog).
fmt    printf-style format string.
\&...    Format arguments (optional).
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
A backtrace needs the binary to be linked with -rdynamic.  The capture
itself can fail, on a platform with no execinfo.h or when an allocation
fails for a moment while symbol names are resolved.  The process still
stops, and the logger writes one "backtrace unavailable" marker line in
place of the frames (a marker record in CLOG_FMT_SYSLOG mode), so you
can see that the backtrace is missing.
When memory stays short for a while, or the content of a record leaves
little room before the internal buffer cap, a backtrace frame that
cannot be formatted is left out the same way, while a shorter frame
after it can still go out.  An incomplete frame is never written, so
every line that goes out is a complete, well-formed record.  When not a
single frame fits, the same "backtrace unavailable" marker takes their
place.  Under the same pressure in CLOG_FMT_JSON mode the backtrace can
fail to fit at all, and a "bt_error":"unavailable" key then takes the
place of "bt", so the omission is visible there too.

On an async logger, clog_fatal first drains everything that earlier
calls already queued, so a message that the application logged moments
before the fatal one reaches the log instead of dying in the queue.
Then the macro writes the fatal record itself synchronously.  Nothing on
that path waits without a bound, and a drain request that the queue
cannot accept is skipped instead of waited on.  The batch of the writer
thread can hold bytes that an earlier write could not deliver (see
clog_create_from_fd_mp(3)).  Those bytes get one bounded attempt, and
after that the library gives up on them: it repairs the framing that a
split record left open and, ahead of the fatal record, writes a "log
record truncated: undelivered batch dropped (records=<n> bytes=<m>):
the process stopped" record for the whole records it gives up on.  A
descriptor that will not take them never holds up the stop.

The fatal record is written on the calling thread.  When the logger
writes to a pipe whose reader is gone, that write fails with EPIPE and
the process still ends through exit(EXIT_FAILURE), because under the
default CLOG_SIGNALS_AUTO policy the library first sets SIGPIPE to
SIG_IGN if its disposition is SIG_DFL at that point.  When the logger
writes to a regular file at the file size limit of the process, the
write fails with EFBIG and the process also ends through
exit(EXIT_FAILURE), since under CLOG_SIGNALS_AUTO the open of the file
already set SIGXFSZ to SIG_IGN if its disposition was SIG_DFL.  Under
CLOG_SIGNALS_UNTOUCHED the disposition that the application set
decides.  See clog_set_signal_policy(3).

clog_fatal ends the process at once, so logger handles that are still
open are not freed one by one; the operating system reclaims every
resource when the program exits.
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
if (!recovered)
    clog_fatal(lg, "unrecoverable state: %s", reason);  /* exits */

if (balance < 0)
    clog_fatal(lg, "invariant violated at step %d", step);  /* exits */
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR clog_alert (3),
.BR clog_error (3),
.BR clog_create_from_fd_mp (3),
.BR clog_create_from_file_mp (3),
.BR clog_set_signal_policy (3)
